    "helps": "when studied material has to last days or weeks: writing out what you remember with the book closed, then checking it against the source in a second colour, beats rereading for the same time on a later test, by a small to moderate margin in this free-recall format, though not on a test minutes away",
    "backfires": "when so little comes back that there is nothing to check, or when the check pass is skipped or scored loosely: errors recalled without correction tend to persist, and people marking their own recall credit wrong answers unless they check it idea by idea"
